// TEST: Intrinsics Inverse Trig Complex
// ============================================================================
// asin/acos/atan on a COMPLEX operand. <complex> overloads all three, so they
// sit in Grad.complexMathIntrinsics and preserve the operand's complex type
// (the emitted call is std::atan(std::complex<double>)) -- unlike floor/ceil
// and log10, which have no complex overload and refuse (intrinsics/013).
// atan(0 + i/2) = i*atanh(1/2) = i*ln(3)/2 is the closed form the pin checks.
//
// Split out of intrinsics/010 on purpose: the INTERPRETER declines complex
// asin/acos/atan as "not yet bit-verified", which makes any file containing one
// a skip in the interpreter differential. Keeping the complex half here leaves
// 010's real-valued pins under that gate.
//
// This file is therefore expected to score SKIP-UNSUPPORTED, never PASS, in
// `blade test interp intrinsics`. It scored FAIL until the decline was moved off
// InterpPanic (BL8011, exit 1 -- a PROGRAM fault) onto the interpreter's
// unsupported channel (Numerics.NumericsUnsupported -> exit 125), which is what
// the runner's skip bucket keys on. If it ever reads PASS, the interpreter grew
// a complex-trig implementation and the claim below needs re-measuring rather
// than celebrating: Numerics.fs records why the libstdc++ closed forms do NOT
// agree with the libm calls the compiled side actually makes.
// ============================================================================
let z = complex(0.0, 0.5)
let za = atan(z)

let w = complex(0.5, 0.0)
let ws = asin(w)
let wc = acos(w)

// wc's imaginary part prints as `-0` (std::acos of a real-valued complex
// lands on negative zero); the pin compares numerically, where -0.0 = 0.0.
// EXPECT: za = (0, 0.549306144334055)
// EXPECT: ws = (0.523598775598299, 0)
// EXPECT: wc = (1.0471975511966, 0)
